Title: MEMENTO MORI
    Episode 1
    THE ORDINARY WRECK
Credit: Created by
Author: Paul Murphy
Source: Based on the original treatment by Paul Murphy
Draft date: 30 October 2026 — pilot-v1
Notes: Development screenplay prepared by the Canyon House Pictures AI team.
    Accountable writer: Charlie Reed (agent).

EXT. COUNTY ROAD / PROPERTY INTAKE - AFTERNOON #1#

A stopped road. Two cars that will not be going anywhere. One has folded its front end around a perfectly ordinary utility pole.

A tow operator sweeps glass. A woman beside the other car explains something to a deputy, then starts the explanation again. Nobody is running.

SUPER: JEFFERSON COUNTY, ALABAMA. 2025.

At a folding table by a county vehicle, RUTH MORI, 85, studies a form. Her funeral clothes have not happened yet. She wears gardening trousers with a clean shirt pulled over them.

An ATTENDANT sets down a clear property bag.

ATTENDANT
Personal effects. The glasses are broken. I put those in the small bag.

RUTH
He has another pair.

The attendant waits. Ruth looks toward the car.

RUTH
At home.

ATTENDANT
You can take your time.

Behind him the tow operator checks his watch. Ruth has seen.

RUTH
Where?

He points to a signature line. She signs. Her hand is steady.

Inside the bag: a pharmacy sack, a takeaway cup with no lid, broken glasses, a wallet, an envelope of discount coupons.

And a small brass-colored plaque on a wooden base.

HUMANITARIAN OF THE YEAR. ELI MORI.

The letters descend slightly toward the right. The word HUMANITARIAN is a different size. An embossed seal has started to peel.

Ruth turns it facedown in the bag.

ATTENDANT
Anything missing that you know of?

RUTH
I don't know what he took.

He nods, reaches for the form.

RUTH
Can I have the pen?

He gives it to her. A cheap county ballpoint. She puts it carefully in her pocket.

The operator lifts the ruined car. A last little spill of glass falls out.

INT. BORROWED CONFERENCE ROOM - AFTERNOON #2#

A clean signature line on a laptop. A cursor waits beneath it.

CALLIE MORI, 53, rewrites a sentence. Her jacket hangs on the chair in a way that suggests it has been there since yesterday.

Across the table, a JUNIOR COLLEAGUE holds two coffees and an argument he is reconsidering.

JUNIOR COLLEAGUE
They asked for assurances.

CALLIE
Then we should assure them we're working on it.

JUNIOR COLLEAGUE
That's what it says.

CALLIE
It says we will obtain approval. Are you the person who approves it?

JUNIOR COLLEAGUE
No.

CALLIE
Then you've written fiction. Good opening. Difficult ending.

She changes WILL OBTAIN to WILL SEEK.

Her phone lights: MOM.

CALLIE
Can you check the dates against the attachments?

She answers, still looking at the sentence.

CALLIE
Hi. I'm in the middle of --

She stops.

The colleague sets down both coffees.

CALLIE
Which hospital?

A long listen.

CALLIE
Were you there?

Another listen. Her free hand finds the edge of the table.

CALLIE
Did they say what happened?

RUTH (V.O.)
He was driving. There was a wreck.

CALLIE
I understand that. I mean --

She does not know what she means.

RUTH (V.O.)
Callie. Can you come?

CALLIE
Of course.

She closes the laptop halfway.

CALLIE
Who's with you?

RUTH (V.O.)
Right now? A man who wants his table back.

Callie almost laughs. The wrong muscle moves in her face.

CALLIE
I'll call Leah. Don't drive if -- I'll call Leah.

The call ends. She opens the laptop again.

JUNIOR COLLEAGUE
I can finish.

CALLIE
There's one sentence.

JUNIOR COLLEAGUE
Callie.

She saves. Sends. Watches the little confirmation appear.

CALLIE
Now you can finish.

She takes the wrong coffee. Leaves the jacket.

INT. CALDWELL KITCHEN - MORNING #3#

A chair rocks on three good legs and an argument. CALEB CALDWELL, 28, a Black mechanic, braces the fourth with his foot while he eats toast.

SUPER: BIRMINGHAM. 1965.

Two bassinets. Two different complaints about the morning.

MARIAN BELL CALDWELL, 25, white, rocks one with a toe while adding a column in a ledger. Her hair is pinned for work. One pin has given up.

CALEB
That one's yours.

MARIAN
They were both mine at three.

CALEB
I was fixing the window.

MARIAN
You were dreaming of fixing the window. I heard you explain it to somebody.

Caleb stands. The chair tips. He catches it without looking.

MARIAN
Don't catch it next time. Then we can get another.

CALEB
It's a good chair.

MARIAN
It's a good rescue.

He wedges a folded matchbook under the leg. The chair settles.

She slides an envelope across the table.

MARIAN
If you're going by the bank.

He opens it. Checks and coins, sorted by name.

CALEB
This yours?

MARIAN
Mrs. Ellis. Mrs. Webb. Mine is the smallest one.

CALEB
I'm not taking their money in there.

MARIAN
The bank or the garage?

CALEB
I'm going to the garage first.

She takes the envelope back. Gives him a different one, thin enough to be insulting.

MARIAN
Then take ours.

He counts the bills again. He knows the answer.

CALEB
Three shifts.

MARIAN
Two and four hours.

CALEB
They took me off Friday.

MARIAN
They didn't pay you Friday. That's a different column.

He looks at the ledger. She turns it so he can see their page. A narrow handwritten line divides it from the clients' accounts.

CALEB
I'll get him to put me back on.

MARIAN
Get what he says on paper.

CALEB
He'll give me paper. He loves giving me paper.

The crying stops on one side. They both look, afraid to disturb the victory.

Caleb bends over a bassinet, barely touches a tiny foot.

MARIAN
Don't start him again.

CALEB
I was congratulating him.

She gives Caleb his lunch. He searches her face for permission to be angry.

MARIAN
Eat that before you go in.

CALEB
Why?

MARIAN
Because I know you.

He kisses her. Leaves with their envelope and their page. The ledger stays.

INT. MORI HOUSE - KITCHEN - EARLY EVENING #4#

SUPER: DAYS LATER. AFTER THE BURIAL.

Food occupies every available surface. A neighbor, ESTHER, 70s, has brought enough potatoes to survive several competing tragedies.

An old prayer book lies open on a low chair in the next room. A mirror is covered by a tablecloth that does not quite reach.

Ruth opens a drawer. The property bag is inside her shopping tote.

She takes out the plaque. Holds it upright for the first time.

Then faces it down in the drawer. A ladle prevents it closing.

ESTHER
Where would he want the mug?

Ruth looks at three mugs lined up by the kettle.

RUTH
What?

ESTHER
For the tea. I thought his mug, but then I thought you might --

RUTH
The chipped one.

ESTHER
They're all chipped.

Ruth removes the ladle. Shuts the drawer. Places the ladle beside the potatoes.

RUTH
Then he left us choices.

Esther reaches toward her, thinks better of it, folds a dish towel instead.

A car door outside.

Ruth smooths the front of her shirt. Only then does she turn.

INT. MORI HOUSE - HALL - CONTINUOUS #5#

CALLIE stands in the doorway holding a small overnight bag and a bag of expensive bread.

BEN MORI, 49, has one wet sleeve and the dead-eyed calm of a man who has explained parking to forty relatives.

BEN
We've decided the bread is the estate.

CALLIE
I didn't know what you needed.

BEN
One person to arrive without bread. We had a chart.

He takes both bags. Hugs her with his dry side.

LEAH MORI, 45, comes down from a stepladder with a screw between her lips.

CALLIE
What happened?

LEAH
Bathroom latch.

CALLIE
It was broken last time.

LEAH
Good. Then it wasn't the funeral.

Callie laughs. Surprises herself.

CALLIE
I can call somebody. You don't have to --

LEAH
It's fixed.

CALLIE
Right.

On a small table: memorial cards, printer cartridges, a photograph facedown.

Callie reaches for the cards.

CALLIE
Is this what you're using?

LEAH
We haven't decided.

CALLIE
I could clean it up.

Leah takes them gently out of her hand.

LEAH
Mom's in the kitchen.

CALLIE
I saw. I was just --

BEN
Go be her daughter. We have hardware covered.

He lifts his sleeve. Water drips on the floor.

CALLIE
Obviously.

For a moment, they are children in the same joke. Then she goes.

INT. MORI HOUSE - KITCHEN - MOMENTS LATER #6#

Ruth and Callie hold each other. Ruth is smaller than Callie expected, and stubborn about being held.

CALLIE
I should have come sooner.

RUTH
You came when you came.

Callie cannot tell if that is mercy.

Esther lifts a casserole with a warning look about its temperature. Callie reaches for the utensil drawer.

Ruth turns too late.

The plaque. Callie lifts it by the wooden base.

CALLIE
Oh, Dad.

Ruth extends her hand.

CALLIE
Humanitarian of the year. Which year?

She tilts it. An engraved date, crooked and wrong.

CALLIE
Apparently two of them. Efficient.

RUTH
Put it back.

CALLIE
Council for Human -- is that a council? This seal's a sticker.

RUTH
I can see it.

CALLIE
You could have warned me. I only brought bread. We could have brought a Nobel.

Esther withdraws with the casserole. Callie notices her going.

RUTH
Your father was a good man.

CALLIE
Good men don't buy their own awards.

Ruth keeps her hand out. Callie hands it over.

CALLIE
Did you know about it?

RUTH
I found it in his car.

CALLIE
That's not what I asked.

RUTH
Then ask me tomorrow.

She puts it in the drawer. Closes it with her hip.

A knock. Ruth takes a breath, changes her face and goes to answer.

Callie stays with the empty space where the award was.

INT. MORI HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - EARLY EVENING #7#

Mourners circulate at the edge of the room. Ruth sits. Ben brings water. Leah has the memorial cards.

RUTH
Not speeches. Something small for the gathering later. Something he did.

BEN
He tried to return a fish.

Leah looks up.

LEAH
It was dead.

BEN
That was the defect.

CALLIE
You can return a dead fish.

BEN
Not from the lake.

Ruth's laugh escapes. She presses her lips together as if she could recall it.

LEAH
He sat outside my test. The whole time. In that terrible car. Wouldn't come in because he thought I'd be embarrassed.

BEN
Were you?

LEAH
Of course. He brought a folding chair.

Ruth looks to Callie.

CALLIE
He promised he'd come to my apartment when I got the first job. I bought six wineglasses because I thought --

She stops. Leah waits.

CALLIE
It's not that kind of story.

RUTH
You don't have to tell one tonight.

CALLIE
I could handle the papers. If there's paperwork you need --

BEN
There it is.

CALLIE
I'm offering.

RUTH
Leon has the will. Another time.

CALLIE
Has he been by?

RUTH
He's coming to sit with us.

Callie nods. A question has found somewhere to go.

INT. FREIGHT DEPOT - SERVICE COUNTER - MORNING (1965) #8#

Behind wire glass, a CLOCK ticks loudly enough to be a policy.

Caleb stands at the service counter. On the other side a CLERK hunts without conviction through a stack of envelopes.

CALEB
Friday. Twelve hours. I did the lift motor on Bay Two.

CLERK
It isn't marked.

CALEB
I know. That's why I'm here.

Through a doorway: the repair garage. A worker Caleb does not recognize rolls his tool trolley across the floor.

Caleb watches it.

CLERK
Mr. Foster handles that.

CALEB
Then let me see him.

CLERK
He's out.

CALEB
I saw his car.

CLERK
I don't keep up with his car.

Caleb sets down the short-pay envelope.

CALEB
I was on next week. Now I'm not on next week. Am I working here or not?

CLERK
I don't make the schedule.

Caleb opens his mouth. Closes it. Points to a carbon sheet beneath the clerk's elbow.

CALEB
That has my name on it.

CLERK
Everybody's name is on it.

CALEB
Cover the others. Give me mine.

The clerk looks at him as though this is troublesome ingenuity.

A MECHANIC Caleb knows passes through the doorway. Caleb turns.

CALEB
Will. You were there Friday.

WILL does not stop walking until he has to.

WILL
I don't do the money.

CALEB
Did I fix the motor?

WILL
You fixed it.

CALEB
Will you tell him?

A pause. The clerk is listening.

WILL
I just did.

He goes. Caleb lets him.

The clerk slides over a carbon with the other names folded under.

CLERK
Best I can do.

Caleb puts it in his envelope. His money stays on the counter a moment longer.

He has to reach back for it.

INT. MORI HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - EVENING #9#

MRS. KAPLAN, 70s, grips a paper plate she has forgotten to fill.

MRS. KAPLAN
He stayed until morning. Everybody else left. Your father stayed.

Ruth takes her hand.

MRS. KAPLAN
My brother couldn't stop asking what it would cost. Eli told him he'd add the breakfast.

CALLIE
When was that?

MRS. KAPLAN
Oh. Years.

CALLIE
Before the old office closed?

MRS. KAPLAN
There were so many offices.

A LAWYER by the doorway, not quietly enough:

LAWYER
There were certainly so many bills.

Callie turns toward him, attracted to the answer she recognizes.

CALLIE
Did he represent your brother, or did he --

MRS. KAPLAN
He stayed.

Callie looks back.

MRS. KAPLAN
You asked when. I don't remember when. I remember the chair. It was orange. He sat in it all night.

She moves her plate to the other hand and sits beside Ruth.

MRS. KAPLAN
I wanted your mother to know that I remember.

Callie has no useful response.

Ben appears with a tower of empty plates.

BEN
Will you take the upper floors?

He transfers half the tower. Across the room somebody laughs at an Eli story they haven't heard.

Callie turns at the sound before she can help it.

EXT. MORI HOUSE - BACK PORCH - EVENING #10#

Callie answers her phone with the plates still in her hand.

CALLIE
Yes. No, I have it.

FIRM PARTNER (V.O.)
We can push the intake to next week. They understand.

CALLIE
They don't know me. That's the problem.

She sets the plates on the railing. One slides; she catches it.

FIRM PARTNER (V.O.)
You've been after this referral for months. It won't evaporate because your father died.

CALLIE
I can give you the assessment tomorrow.

FIRM PARTNER (V.O.)
That's not what I'm asking.

CALLIE
It's what I'm answering.

Ben opens the door. Sees the call, starts to retreat.

CALLIE
I'll send it before lunch. Thank you.

She hangs up.

BEN
Was that work?

CALLIE
No. An extremely formal lover.

BEN
Since you're doing that, the insurance thing -- Mom doesn't know where he kept --

CALLIE
Don't.

BEN
I was asking if --

CALLIE
If I could do the part where I know what everything means. Yes. Everyone's favorite part of me.

Ben looks down at his sleeve. Still wet.

BEN
I canceled two jobs. Leah's got patients moved around through Friday. We didn't make an announcement.

CALLIE
I didn't ask you to.

BEN
No. You didn't ask.

He picks up the plates.

BEN
You want a towel? It's cold out here.

CALLIE
I'm fine.

BEN
I wasn't proposing treatment. Your hands are wet.

He goes inside. Callie wipes them on her expensive trousers.

INT. SMALL LAW OFFICE - WORKROOM - LATE MORNING (1965) #11#

A small room doing too much. A desk, a typewriter, shelves with missing dividers. A fan moves warm air from one person to another.

YOUNG LEON, 31, a Black attorney, gives an OLDER CLIENT a corrected letter.

YOUNG LEON
Take that one. The first copy says Tuesday. You told me Thursday.

OLDER CLIENT
You believe me now?

YOUNG LEON
I believed you Tuesday.

The client laughs. Caleb steps aside to let her out.

YOUNG ELI MORI, 29, a white Jewish attorney, comes from the adjoining office with his sleeves rolled up and a tie that has survived an argument.

YOUNG ELI
Caleb Caldwell?

Caleb stands. Eli's hand is offered too quickly. Caleb takes it at his own speed.

YOUNG ELI
Leon says you're being deprived of your livelihood.

CALEB
I said they owe me money.

YOUNG LEON
Sit down. We'll begin with what you said.

Caleb lays out the envelope and carbon. Eli sits across from him; Leon keeps the side chair.

CALEB
Twenty-eight hours. They paid for twelve. Then my shifts came off the board.

YOUNG ELI
Who took them off?

CALEB
Foster runs the floor. He said there wasn't the work.

YOUNG ELI
Is there?

CALEB
Somebody was doing it this morning.

Eli writes quickly.

YOUNG ELI
What changed?

CALEB
My wife came to bring my lunch. He saw her.

Leon watches Caleb, not Eli's pen.

YOUNG ELI
Your wife is white.

CALEB
Yes.

YOUNG ELI
And after that --

CALEB
After that, what I told you. That's what I know.

Eli checks himself. Writes a smaller note.

YOUNG LEON
What do you want to happen?

CALEB
Pay me. Put me on the board.

YOUNG ELI
And when they do it again?

CALEB
I'd like to get paid this time before we discuss next time.

Eli smiles. Caleb does not join him yet.

YOUNG ELI
There could be a larger use for this. A company depends on people not hearing certain things about it.

CALEB
It depends on the trucks working. That's why they hired me.

YOUNG LEON
Who can speak to the missing shifts?

CALEB
A man who wouldn't stay long enough to say his name this morning.

YOUNG ELI
Your household income. Your wife works?

CALEB
Books. Accounts. Mrs. Ellis and Mrs. Webb, some church work. They pay her.

Eli writes the names. Caleb notices the speed but not the significance.

CALEB
She's got all our numbers.

YOUNG ELI
I'd like her to bring them.

CALEB
I'll ask her.

YOUNG ELI
Good. We can make them uncomfortable.

YOUNG LEON
And tomorrow he has to walk back in there.

Eli looks at Leon. Then at Caleb.

CALEB
I want a job I can go back to. Not a story about why I had to leave.

Eli puts down his pen. Gives that its due.

INT. MORI HOUSE - FRONT HALL - EVENING #12#

LEON, 91, waits while a DRIVER folds a light walking frame by the porch. Leon keeps a cane. The driver offers an elbow; Leon accepts it for the step.

Once inside, he releases the arm and takes his own time.

Ruth meets him. They touch foreheads briefly. No performance.

RUTH
That tie.

LEON
He hated it.

RUTH
I know.

He has a slim leather case. Callie reaches for it.

CALLIE
I can take that.

LEON
You can point me at a chair.

Leah appears with a dining chair that has arms.

LEAH
This one. It's higher.

LEON
An excellent reason to choose anything.

She sets it down. He sits, breathes, arranges the case against his own leg.

CALLIE
Did you bring the will?

RUTH
Callie.

LEON
Yes.

CALLIE
Mom said you'd handle it. I thought I could help.

LEON
You can help me with my coat.

She does. Carefully. The intimacy unsettles her more than the rebuff.

LEON
Thank you. Now tell me how the service was.

Callie looks to Ruth. Ruth sits beside him.

RUTH
Too many people stood up. He would have enjoyed that.

Leon smiles at her. Callie stands holding his coat.

INT. CALDWELL KITCHEN - MIDDAY (1965) #13#

MRS. ELLIS, Black, 40s, empties a handbag onto Marian's work table. Receipts, keys, a sweet wrapped in paper.

MRS. ELLIS
I had it separate.

MARIAN
Separate from what?

MRS. ELLIS
From everything I could find.

Marian sorts. A baby sleeps against her shoulder.

MRS. ELLIS
We could get the books Saturday. Mr. Thomas has a car.

MARIAN
We have nine dollars in promises.

MRS. ELLIS
Good promises.

MARIAN
The book man takes the other kind.

She points to the ledger. Not Caleb's page. Clients' accounts, the reading fund kept apart.

MARIAN
Mrs. Webb gave for the light. That stays for the light.

MRS. ELLIS
If we don't open, we won't need one.

Caleb comes in with the carbon.

CALEB
They want to talk this afternoon. Both of us.

MARIAN
Who?

CALEB
Leon. The other fellow. Eli.

MRS. ELLIS
You found lawyers?

CALEB
They have a desk.

He puts the carbon on a clear corner.

CALEB
I told them you keep the books. Bring the ledger, they can see how --

Marian closes it. Quietly.

MARIAN
I'll bring our page.

CALEB
They asked about the whole picture.

MARIAN
Mrs. Ellis isn't in our picture.

MRS. ELLIS
I hate pictures. Always catch me with my eyes shut.

The joke gives Caleb somewhere to put his embarrassment.

CALEB
I meant the numbers.

MARIAN
Then they'll get ours.

Mrs. Ellis retrieves her keys, leaves the sweet beside Marian's hand. A small fee for holding the line.

INT. MORI HOUSE - OFFICE HALL - EVENING #14#

Leon moves toward Eli's office, using the wall briefly while he changes the cane to his other hand. The leather case stays with him.

Callie opens the door before he reaches it.

CALLIE
Mom told me not to go in.

LEON
Then don't.

CALLIE
But you can.

LEON
I left something here.

He sits in the desk chair before opening a shallow drawer. An unmarked sealed packet. He checks its edges and places it in his case.

CALLIE
What is that?

LEON
Instructions for me.

CALLIE
From my father.

LEON
Yes.

CALLIE
About my father's estate.

LEON
Some of them.

She stands in the doorway. Not blocking him quite yet.

CALLIE
I'm fifty-three. You don't have to do the grown-ups-are-talking voice.

LEON
I have the voice I have.

That lands. She steps away from the doorway.

CALLIE
If you brought the will, we should hear it. So Mom doesn't have to do this twice.

Ruth has come up behind her.

RUTH
I have been doing things twice all week.

CALLIE
I'm trying to --

RUTH
After they leave the dining room. Briefly. Nobody takes anything tonight.

LEON
I can explain the copy. That is all.

Callie nods. A win, or what she knows how to call one.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - LATER #15#

The last condolence cake is carried out. Leah wipes a ring off the table. Callie sets down her laptop.

RUTH
No recording.

CALLIE
It's for notes.

RUTH
I said no recording.

Callie turns the screen around. An empty document. Ruth leaves it there.

Leon settles into the armed chair. Leah places a glass of water within easy reach. He moves it an inch from his papers.

BEN
Mom's checking account is all right? For the house. The ordinary things.

LEON
We should list the questions for the person handling the estate. This is a copy Eli asked me to explain to you. I'm not distributing money tonight.

BEN
I don't mean distributing. I mean the electric bill.

CALLIE
Which account is it paid from?

RUTH
Mine.

BEN
Good. Good. So we don't have to --

RUTH
I have paid an electric bill before.

Ben sits. Callie types a heading.

LEON
Some of the property is described in separate papers. There are named trustees. There are questions I won't answer off the top of my head.

CALLIE
That'll be a first for this table.

Nobody laughs. She looks down.

LEON
Would you like me to begin?

Ruth nods. Leon opens the case. For a moment he leaves his hand resting on it.

LEON
He was very pleased with himself.

BEN
Finally. Something we can all stipulate to.

This time Ruth almost smiles.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - CONTINUOUS #16#

Objects emerge from the case and a small box Ruth has brought: a watch, a fountain pen in its worn sleeve, a file cover, a broken chess clock. A folded photograph. A small menorah.

Ben puts on the watch. It hangs loosely.

BEN
He left me punctuality. A thing he never used.

LEON
There's a note with it.

BEN
Of course there is.

Leon offers the note. Ben looks, then folds it without sharing.

CALLIE
What does it say?

BEN
It's with my watch.

Callie lets that go. Not gracefully.

Leah tests the chess clock. One side runs. The other does not.

LEAH
I fixed this.

BEN
Then sue the estate.

LEAH
We fixed it. It had a spring --

She turns it over. A small rectangle of old tape on the bottom.

LEAH
I put that there.

Callie is passed the pen and worn file cover. She looks inside the cover. Empty.

CALLIE
He's left me paperwork without paperwork. Very advanced.

LEON
The contents are not all here.

CALLIE
Of course they aren't.

She puts the pen down. Ben reaches for the folded photograph, but Ruth moves it beneath the inventory sheet as she clears space for Leon's water.

It remains closed.

BEN
Who had the menorah?

RUTH
We did.

LEAH
I've never seen it.

RUTH
You haven't seen everything we owned.

BEN
Did he leave us any furniture we recognize?

Callie tries the empty cover against the inventory. A puzzle fit. Leah watches her do it.

LEAH
Could you leave that alone for a minute?

CALLIE
I'm looking.

LEAH
I know. You make it loud.

INT. CHURCH WORKROOM - AFTERNOON (1965) #17#

Marian holds one end of a tape measure. Mrs. Ellis holds the other with insufficient enthusiasm for accuracy.

MRS. ELLIS
That shelf's going to fit.

MARIAN
If we move the door.

MRS. ELLIS
We could do that.

MRS. WEBB, Black, 50s, sorts donated books at the table. She lifts a huge engineering manual.

MRS. WEBB
Here's one for the little ones.

Marian takes the book. Opens it. A hundred pages of diagrams.

MARIAN
Caleb might like that.

MRS. WEBB
He's not getting it. It's a donation.

Caleb appears in the doorway. Puts his hands up.

CALEB
I didn't come to steal the literature.

Mrs. Webb hands him the heavy book. He turns it over, impressed.

Marian puts two slim children's books on a shelf marked by pencil.

MRS. ELLIS
We can't open with two.

MARIAN
Four. The other two are being mended.

MRS. ELLIS
Four books. We'll need a queue.

CALEB
I can do the shelves. Give me a piece of wood that used to be something else.

MRS. WEBB
There's a crate behind the room.

CALEB
A crate wants to be a shelf. You can hear it.

Marian takes the engineering book back before he absently carries it out.

MARIAN
We start with the little table. We open when I said. We don't spend the light money.

MRS. ELLIS
You don't have to win every column.

That stings because it is true.

Marian changes the penciled shelf length. Hands Mrs. Ellis the tape.

MARIAN
Then you choose where the table goes.

Mrs. Ellis immediately moves it. Marian stops herself correcting her.

Caleb sees the effort. Gives her a tiny approving nod.

She takes her handbag. They leave the books where other people can decide about them.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - EVENING #18#

Leon has reached a page with several attached schedules.

LEON
There's a storage unit listed here. Collectively for the family, in his language.

BEN
What's in it?

LEON
I haven't inventoried it for you.

CALLIE
Who has?

LEON
I don't know.

She types. A real unknown does not satisfy her.

LEON
The papers name a fund and two trusts. You have copies of the names. Those need to be read with the separate documents.

CALLIE
This foundation. Do you recognize it?

Ben and Leah shake their heads. Ruth studies her hands.

BEN
He never mentioned anything with that many nouns.

CALLIE
He mentioned things with exactly that many nouns. That's how he got through Thanksgiving.

LEON
He also asks that the family have dinner together after shiva.

Ben looks around at the table, food still at its far end.

BEN
Do we get credit for this?

LEON
He had a particular dinner in mind.

CALLIE
With an agenda?

LEON
With people.

LEAH
Do we have to?

LEON
Your father wanted it. You can decide what you do with that.

BEN
Did he reserve? Because if he reserved for eight, I don't want to be the one who --

RUTH
Ben.

BEN
I'm trying to see the evening.

Callie holds out her hand for the schedule. Leon passes a copy, keeps his own.

CALLIE
We need the whole financial picture. The balances, who controls these, who --

LEON
You need more than I can hand you from this case.

CALLIE
Then give me what you can.

He looks at the page waiting beneath his hand.

LEON
That is what I'm doing.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - MOMENTS LATER #19#

The room has tired. Ruth's eyes are closed, but she is not asleep.

LEON
There are named beneficiaries we haven't discussed. Daniel Caldwell. And Sarah Caldwell.

Ruth looks down at Daniel. Before Sarah.

Callie notices.

BEN
Who?

LEON
Daniel and Sarah Caldwell.

BEN
I heard the names.

Callie finds them on the schedule. Each has a line. Balanced. Deliberate.

CALLIE
Are they related to us?

Leon takes a sip of water.

CALLIE
Leon.

LEON
They're people your father knew.

LEAH
Clients?

LEON
They can tell you about themselves.

BEN
Do they know they're in this?

LEON
I've been in touch.

CALLIE
How old are they?

RUTH
Callie.

CALLIE
It's a question.

LEON
They are adults. They're twins.

The air changes. Callie looks from Leon to Ruth. Nobody was surprised at the same time she was.

CALLIE
Mom?

Ruth raises her eyes. She knows what Callie has decided.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - CONTINUOUS #20#

RUTH
Don't do it this way.

CALLIE
What way would you recommend?

LEAH
What are we doing?

CALLIE
Apparently we're meeting the other family through a schedule.

Ben looks at Ruth. Not accusing yet. Worse: waiting.

RUTH
You don't know these people.

CALLIE
You do.

Silence.

CALLIE
You knew before tonight.

RUTH
Yes.

Ben takes off the watch. Lays it beside his plate.

LEAH
Mom, what does that mean?

RUTH
It means I know them. It does not mean your sister gets to make them whatever she needs.

CALLIE
I need you to say I'm wrong.

RUTH
You need a great many things said exactly as you write them.

CALLIE
Did he give them money while you were trying to --

LEON
Enough.

It is not loud. It stops her.

LEON
I should not have let this become this.

CALLIE
Then finish it.

She reaches for the sealed packet at the side of his case.

Leon lays a hand on it. No struggle. His authority is in the fact that he need not hurry.

LEON
That is mine to open.

CALLIE
For him.

LEON
Yes.

CALLIE
He's dead.

Leon looks at her. Ruth gets up. Leah starts toward her; Ruth stops her with a small gesture and leaves alone.

Ben stares at the watch. Callie waits for someone to thank her for asking.

Nobody does.

INT. MORI HOUSE - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS #21#

Ruth transfers food from a large container to a smaller one. There is no need to do it now.

Callie enters.

CALLIE
You don't have to cover for him anymore.

RUTH
Hold that.

Callie holds the small container.

CALLIE
Whatever you decided when we were children, we're not children now.

RUTH
I have noticed. You're extremely difficult to dress.

CALLIE
I'm serious.

RUTH
So am I. The lid.

Callie hands it over.

CALLIE
I'll deal with it. You don't have to be the person who explains.

RUTH
You have already explained.

CALLIE
Because no one else will.

Ruth presses the lid. One corner will not catch. She presses again.

CALLIE
Here.

She reaches. Ruth pulls it toward herself.

RUTH
Did you come to sit with me?

CALLIE
Of course.

RUTH
When?

The refrigerator hums between them.

Callie looks at the drawer. Ruth follows the look and pushes it firmly closed with her hand.

RUTH
Go ask your questions somewhere else for five minutes.

Callie leaves. Ruth finally gets the lid to catch. It splits down the middle.

INT. CALDWELL KITCHEN - LATE AFTERNOON (1965) #22#

One baby howls. The other joins, offended to have missed a decision.

Caleb stands ready by the door. Marian has her handbag on her shoulder and both hands occupied.

CALEB
They'll close.

MARIAN
Then take him.

She gives him a baby. The child quiets. Victory.

MARIAN
Now take her.

CALEB
I only just --

She places the second safely against his other arm. He adjusts, very careful.

Marian takes off her handbag. Sits at the ledger. Copies their household figures onto a separate sheet.

CALEB
We could have brought the book.

MARIAN
And I could have brought the stove. This is what they need.

He looks at the children. One has his finger. He cannot hurry anything now.

CALEB
He thinks they can make enough noise to get Foster to listen.

MARIAN
Which one?

CALEB
Eli.

MARIAN
He has enough noise of his own.

CALEB
You haven't met him.

MARIAN
You've described him.

She marks a column, checks it again.

CALEB
I don't want them growing up thinking every time somebody shuts a door we were grateful to find a window.

Marian stops writing. Gives him the whole thought, not a quick objection.

MARIAN
I don't either.

CALEB
Then --

MARIAN
I want them growing up. In a house we can pay for. With work I didn't have to ask your employer to let me keep.

CALEB
I'm not asking them permission for you.

MARIAN
Then don't offer my people with the numbers.

He looks at her. She has seen more than he told her.

CALEB
I said Mrs. Ellis and Mrs. Webb. I was trying to show we weren't just sitting here --

MARIAN
I know what you were trying to show.

She closes the ledger. Puts it high on a kitchen shelf.

He waits while she gets the children's things ready. The pause costs them minutes. He lets it.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - EVENING #23#

Callie lays out the offered copies, squares them with the table's edge. Ben watches the transformation of dinner into a workstation.

BEN
Can my glass be evidence or should I move it?

CALLIE
You can move it.

He doesn't.

She photographs a page Ben has given her.

LEAH
Not mine.

CALLIE
I'm comparing the schedules.

LEAH
Then compare Ben's schedule.

Callie leaves Leah's paper alone. A tiny concession made conspicuous.

BEN
If you find out the watch is worth something, don't start with that.

CALLIE
Why would I?

BEN
Because it will sound like that was what I asked.

Callie looks at him properly. He has not put the watch back on.

CALLIE
Did you need it to be money?

BEN
I needed it to be something without homework.

He leaves his copy. Leah remains standing.

Callie's screen has a heading: OTHER FAMILY.

LEAH
Take that off.

CALLIE
It's just a working --

LEAH
Take it off.

Callie adds a question mark.

LEAH
They have names. Start there.

CALLIE
Do you have a different explanation?

LEAH
No. It doesn't hurt me to say that.

She takes the chess clock and her paper. Leaves Callie with what was actually offered.

INT. MORI HOUSE - ELI'S OFFICE - EVENING #24#

Ruth opens the door. Callie has asked for an old family folder; Ruth points to a shallow shelf.

RUTH
Blue. Not the green one.

The office is disappointing. Dust, spare reading glasses, a dead plant which could have died in any administration.

Callie takes the blue folder. Ruth stays in the doorway.

CALLIE
You can sit.

RUTH
I was sitting. You wanted this.

Inside: school documents, cards, photocopies. Callie's name in several decades of handwriting.

An application to an arts administration program. CALLIE MORI. Her young handwriting, confident in a way her present handwriting isn't.

A clipped page in Eli's hand. LAW APPLICATION DATES. A number circled twice. A line beside a scholarship possibility.

Callie pulls the clip off.

CALLIE
You kept this.

RUTH
He kept it.

CALLIE
I didn't even send it.

RUTH
I know.

CALLIE
You said I could do it later.

RUTH
You were good at the law.

CALLIE
I know that too.

Ruth has no useful answer. Callie replaces the clip. Exactly where it was.

She sees a lower cabinet. Tries its handle. Locked.

RUTH
The folder.

Callie lets go of the handle.

CALLIE
I wanted to know if there were family papers.

RUTH
You are holding family papers.

Callie leaves with the folder. Ruth switches off the light before she follows.

INT. SMALL LAW OFFICE - WORKROOM - LATE AFTERNOON (1965) #25#

Young Eli has a typed page ready. Too ready.

Marian reads while Caleb studies the pen in Eli's hand. Leon has put their single household sheet beside the pay carbon.

YOUNG ELI
It's a first account. Nothing has been sent.

MARIAN
Mrs. Ellis?

YOUNG ELI
Your husband mentioned people who could speak to the household circumstances.

MARIAN
She can speak to Mrs. Ellis's circumstances.

Caleb shifts.

YOUNG ELI
The point is to show the cost. They call it scheduling. We give it a face.

MARIAN
I brought mine.

Eli looks up. A beat before he appreciates the objection.

YOUNG ELI
Yes. And if we can show the number of people affected --

MARIAN
Affected by what?

YOUNG ELI
What they're doing to your husband.

MARIAN
Then write about my husband.

She reads another line. Looks at Leon.

MARIAN
This has our address.

YOUNG LEON
It does.

YOUNG ELI
It would have to be a full account eventually. For the moment I'm imagining --

MARIAN
Where does this go?

He stops on that. The first question with an answer he must choose.

YOUNG ELI
Nowhere until we decide.

YOUNG LEON
All of us.

Marian lays the page down. Not relinquishing it. Marking the place where it stopped.

EXT. MORI HOUSE - BACKYARD - NIGHT #26#

Leah has put several family photographs on a dry garden table. One solar light gives them a greenish cast.

Callie comes out with her phone.

LEAH
Not a trick. I actually need a second pair of eyes.

She shows Callie a photograph of Eli and the three children. Callie is taller than Ben and already trying to organize him. Leah is small, clutching Eli's trouser leg.

CALLIE
I remember that shirt.

LEAH
Which one?

CALLIE
His. He bought three. Said he'd found his system.

LEAH
One shirt had to work six days.

Callie smiles. A real one.

Leah puts another photo beside it. Eli leans over the chessboard with Ben.

CALLIE
He used to take my rook while he asked me about school.

LEAH
He did that to me.

CALLIE
You let him?

LEAH
I started keeping my rooks in my pocket.

They laugh. Leah's laugh breaks first. She presses her thumb into the table.

Callie reaches toward her. Her phone vibrates. She looks at it.

Leah sees the look.

LEAH
Choose one.

CALLIE
I don't want to make a saint out of him.

LEAH
It's a picture. He's wearing a terrible shirt. Nobody's going to canonize him.

CALLIE
If we find out tomorrow --

LEAH
Tomorrow can have a picture too.

She hands over the photo of all four. Callie takes it.

LEAH
He sat outside my test. That happened. Whatever else you find, it already happened.

Callie looks toward the lit dining room. Her pages waiting.

CALLIE
I just need to finish something.

LEAH
I know.

Leah gathers the other photos. Alone now, although Callie has not yet walked away.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM / KITCHEN THRESHOLD - NIGHT #27#

Leon has dozed in his chair, one hand on the case. He opens his eyes before Callie speaks.

LEON
I wasn't asleep for the whole thing.

CALLIE
You missed the encore.

She sits, makes an effort not to begin standing over him.

CALLIE
Will you ask them to talk to me?

LEON
I can pass on a request.

CALLIE
Or give me a number. Adults, as you pointed out.

LEON
You can wait for an answer.

CALLIE
I didn't say I couldn't.

Ruth, in the kitchen threshold, has heard.

RUTH
I have Sarah's number.

Callie turns. Leon closes his eyes for a moment.

RUTH
I don't want you turning up at a door.

CALLIE
I wasn't going to.

RUTH
Then I am delighted to prevent something.

She opens her phone. Struggles with the brightness, not the decision. Hands it to Callie with the number displayed.

CALLIE
Sarah Caldwell. In your contacts.

RUTH
Yes.

Callie copies it.

RUTH
Ask if she wants to talk. Ask. Don't explain her to her.

CALLIE
I make phone calls for a living.

LEON
This woman doesn't work for you.

Callie looks at the two of them, allied without a meeting.

CALLIE
I'm beginning to see that no one does.

She takes her phone outside. Ruth watches her go, regretting a choice she made herself.

EXT. MORI HOUSE - FRONT WALK - NIGHT #28#

Callie walks far enough from the door to be private, close enough to see her own coat hanging in the hall.

Voicemail tone.

CALLIE
Sarah. This is Callie Mori. Eli's daughter. My mother gave me your number.

She stops pacing. Finds her professional voice.

CALLIE
We heard the will tonight. Obviously there are things my family hasn't been told. I'm not interested in making this unpleasant. I'd rather we talked directly.

The front door opens. Ruth appears with a cardigan. Callie turns away so her mother cannot interrupt.

CALLIE
If my father had another family, I'd prefer we didn't have to discover each other through his lawyer.

Ruth stops on the step.

Callie hears her own sentence a fraction too late.

CALLIE
I'm not saying -- I know this is difficult. You have my number now. Please call me.

She ends the message. Nothing to press to take it back.

Ruth holds out the cardigan. Callie takes it.

RUTH
Your father had several cardigans too.

She goes inside. Callie stands in the cold with one more thing she has been given.

INT. SMALL LAW OFFICE - WORKROOM - EARLY EVENING (1965) #29#

The fan has stopped. Nobody has noticed until now.

Eli plugs it into another socket. It starts, blowing the typed page toward Marian. She catches it.

MARIAN
These two names come off.

YOUNG ELI
They're people who know you. We wouldn't use anything without speaking to --

MARIAN
Then speak to them before they're on a list.

She puts her hand beside the address.

MARIAN
And this doesn't go out.

CALEB
They know where we live.

MARIAN
Foster knows. Whoever gets a copy doesn't need directions.

Eli takes a pencil.

YOUNG ELI
We can make a version for a private approach. But at some point, if you want pressure, we can't whisper everything.

CALEB
That's what I've been saying.

MARIAN
You can say it with your name. Not Mrs. Ellis's. Not with my accounts.

YOUNG ELI
We're discussing possible support. I'm not asking for her bankbook.

MARIAN
Good. You won't get it.

Eli sits back. His charm, for once, has nowhere to go.

CALEB
Can we have a minute?

Leon starts to get up. Marian shakes her head.

MARIAN
They can hear me. They've already written about me.

Caleb lowers his voice anyway.

CALEB
We finally get somebody to listen, and all we're doing is telling him what he can't say.

MARIAN
He can say twenty-eight hours. He can say twelve paid. He can say you were taken off the board. He's got a whole page left.

CALEB
It's not only the hours.

MARIAN
I was there when he looked at me.

Quiet. She does not have to describe the look.

MARIAN
I know what you mean.

CALEB
Then stop making it sound like I want a little show. I want to walk in there and not have to pretend I live alone.

She lets that hurt her.

MARIAN
And I want to go do my work tomorrow without wondering who you've asked them to call.

CALEB
You keep talking about tomorrow like it's somewhere I don't have to get to.

MARIAN
I keep talking about it because I have to get there too.

She separates Eli's list from their household sheet. The clients' ledger is not here to take.

MARIAN
Our page stays. Our money. Our account. You can ask us what else you need. You can't decide the rest is already yours.

Eli looks at Caleb for an answer. Caleb sees him doing it.

CALEB
She said ask us.

Leon gets up, slowly, and opens the corridor door.

YOUNG LEON
Caleb. Help me with something.

Eli looks toward the door. Leon is not asking him.

INT. SMALL LAW OFFICE - CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS (1965) #30#

Caleb follows Leon into a narrow passage. Leon hands him an empty water jug.

CALEB
What needs help?

YOUNG LEON
The jug's a courtesy. You can leave it.

Caleb sets it down.

CALEB
You going to tell me she's right?

YOUNG LEON
You heard what she said.

CALEB
I hear everybody. I hear him. I hear her. I hear Foster telling me nothing while somebody else does my work.

He rubs his palms. Garage grime remains in the creases despite washing.

CALEB
I thought you might say what to do.

YOUNG LEON
I can tell you what a paper asks for. I can't tell you what going home with it feels like.

CALEB
You don't think I should fight.

YOUNG LEON
I didn't say that.

CALEB
You can say it plain. I can take it.

Leon leans against the wall. Lets Caleb stop performing readiness.

YOUNG LEON
I'd like this to be the case that makes somebody change a thing. I'd like to look at a result and know I helped get it. That's not all caution in there.

Caleb listens.

YOUNG LEON
But I have a chair to come back to. A room with my papers in it. You go out of here and ask somebody for your wages. She goes out of here and keeps other people's money straight. We don't all take the same thing home.

CALEB
I don't want to go home with less every time.

YOUNG LEON
Then don't make her agree to less so you can call it more.

Caleb looks at the open door. Marian's voice, too low to make out. She is still working on it.

CALEB
He always have something ready before the question?

YOUNG LEON
Usually two things.

Caleb picks up the jug.

CALEB
Might as well fill it.

Leon steps aside. Caleb goes toward the sink, then back toward the room. He has chosen to return.

INT. MORI HOUSE - KITCHEN - LATE NIGHT #31#

Ruth sits at the table with the plaque in front of her, face up. Callie comes in for water. Stops.

RUTH
It's not going to grow a new name if you look at it longer.

CALLIE
I was getting water.

Ruth points to the cupboard. Callie gets a glass. Runs the tap much longer than necessary.

CALLIE
I shouldn't have said it in front of everyone.

RUTH
You said it outside as well. Very thorough.

CALLIE
Then I shouldn't have said it that way.

RUTH
You keep moving the apology an inch to the left.

Callie sits.

CALLIE
How long have you known them?

RUTH
Before tonight.

CALLIE
Mom.

RUTH
That was your question at the table. There's your answer.

CALLIE
I don't understand why you're protecting him from something he's apparently announcing.

RUTH
I'm sitting in my kitchen. You're the one who put him on trial.

Callie looks at the plaque.

CALLIE
There isn't one thing I can take at face value. Even the stupid award has an argument attached.

Ruth turns it over only far enough to move it closer. The back remains unseen.

CALLIE
Tell me one thing.

RUTH
I have told you things all evening.

CALLIE
Something that wasn't managed. One thing he did without a reason tucked underneath.

Ruth thinks. It takes long enough that Callie regrets asking.

RUTH
He heated my shoes.

CALLIE
What?

RUTH
When I had to leave early. I hated the cold shoes. He put them by the vent before I got up.

Callie waits for the larger meaning.

RUTH
Sometimes he got up too late. Then he'd put the hair dryer in them. Ruined one pair.

A little smile. It goes before Callie can interrogate it.

CALLIE
He never --

She stops.

RUTH
I know. That's yours. This one's mine.

Callie drinks. Ruth puts the plaque in her shopping bag and sets the bag between her feet.

CALLIE
Where are you taking it?

RUTH
With me.

She gets up with the bag. Callie rises to help. Ruth accepts the hand for balance, then keeps the bag herself.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - LATE NIGHT #32#

Leon closes his case. His phone shows a message from the driver. He types a short answer with one finger.

Callie comes in with the glass.

CALLIE
I'd like to see the storage unit.

LEON
Then ask about a visit.

CALLIE
I'm asking.

LEON
You're asking me at midnight with your coat already on. I'm not taking you anywhere.

CALLIE
The key, then.

He looks at her. She takes off the cardigan, irritated to have proved him right.

CALLIE
He named us collectively. I'm one of us.

LEON
And the others?

CALLIE
I'll talk to them.

LEON
That sounds like a thing you haven't done.

She sits opposite. It is harder from eye level.

CALLIE
If the papers don't make sense, I can challenge them.

LEON
You can speak to a lawyer who isn't sitting inside her own family.

CALLIE
I am a lawyer.

LEON
I remember. Your parents were unbearable about it.

Despite herself, she smiles. He doesn't reward the smile by yielding.

CALLIE
What happens to your packet if I do?

LEON
Those are two different questions.

CALLIE
They're next to each other on his page.

LEON
That doesn't make them the same question.

He rests his hand on the case.

LEON
He thought someone might formally challenge the will. He left me instructions for that possibility. About material I should then consider releasing.

CALLIE
So he wrote a will he expected us to fight.

LEON
He expected you to react.

CALLIE
And you agreed to keep score.

He sits back. That was deliberately cruel. She sees the hit and wants the advantage.

CALLIE
If a challenge makes you open it, why don't we save ourselves the --

LEON
It does not make me do anything for you tonight.

Silence.

LEON
You want advice about a challenge, get advice. You want a visit, ask the people involved. You don't get to frighten me into combining them.

CALLIE
I'm not trying to frighten you.

LEON
Then you should be relieved it isn't working.

His phone lights. He turns it over without answering yet.

INT. SMALL LAW OFFICE - WORKROOM - EVENING (1965) #33#

Eli feeds a fresh sheet into the typewriter. Gets it crooked. Pulls it out and starts again.

Caleb puts down the filled jug. Marian has their household paper on her lap.

YOUNG ELI
Private meeting. First, the pay entries. Dates. Hours. Who told you you weren't on the schedule.

He types. The keys are louder than he intends.

YOUNG ELI
No list of other households.

MARIAN
Clients.

YOUNG ELI
No list of your clients.

He crosses out the names on the first draft. Does not make a joke about it.

CALEB
And then?

YOUNG ELI
Then we decide what can be asked for and what you're willing to do to ask it.

CALEB
That's smaller than when I came in.

YOUNG LEON
It's clearer.

CALEB
Clear doesn't pay me Friday.

The two lawyers let that remain true.

Marian looks at the fresh page.

MARIAN
I can give you our figures. I won't bring the ledger.

YOUNG ELI
All right.

She studies his face. Waiting for the condition.

YOUNG ELI
All right, Marian. The figures.

Caleb straightens his carbon. Something to do with his hands while he accepts a smaller move.

CALEB
I don't have the exact dates for every shift he changed.

YOUNG LEON
Mark which ones you know. Leave the others until you do.

CALEB
I know I was there.

YOUNG LEON
Then we'll work on when.

Eli rolls out the new page. Sets it between Caleb and Marian, not before Caleb alone.

YOUNG ELI
Read it before I make the copy.

They bend over it together. She points to a number. He corrects it in his own hand.

Marian passes back their agreed household sheet. Puts the excluded list into her handbag.

The broader unsigned draft remains on Eli's desk, its crossed-out names visible. A proposal that did not go anywhere today.

YOUNG LEON
Tomorrow afternoon?

MARIAN
After four. I have paid work.

CALEB
After four.

Eli writes the time. This time, after asking.

INT. MORI HOUSE - FRONT HALL - LATE NIGHT #34#

The driver waits outside. Leah has brought Leon's coat. He takes a moment standing, then accepts her arm for the turn toward the door.

Callie holds the coat open for his other shoulder.

CALLIE
You used to tell me what he meant.

LEON
Sometimes I told you what would make it easier.

CALLIE
That's the same thing, isn't it? With him.

LEON
No.

She smooths the collar. He cannot pretend not to feel her hands.

CALLIE
What were you making easier tonight?

LEON
I didn't make tonight easier.

CALLIE
You could still --

LEON
Callie. I am going home.

His case is by his leg. The driver picks it up only when Leon nods.

Ruth appears with a container of food, its cracked lid secured by elastic bands.

RUTH
Potatoes. There may be a chicken underneath.

LEON
Then I have plans for tomorrow.

RUTH
Call me when you get there.

LEON
I will.

He touches her hand. Accepts the driver's arm for the step. Keeps deciding the pace.

Callie watches the case go to the car. The door closes on a thing she has not obtained.

EXT. CALDWELL STREET / INT. CALDWELL KITCHEN - NIGHT (1965) #35#

Caleb and Marian come home carrying their papers. A neighbor, MR. HAYES, wrestles a gate that will not latch.

MR. HAYES
It'll do it till somebody's watching. Then it's a good gate.

Caleb takes the latch. Feels the drag.

CALEB
Got a nail?

MR. HAYES
I've got four. I can spare one.

Marian goes inside to relieve the woman minding the babies. Caleb kneels by the gate.

LATER: Inside, the children sleep. Marian checks the envelope for the woman who helped. Pays her before checking what remains.

Caleb carries in two salvaged boards from the workroom crate. Sets them on a towel on the table.

MARIAN
Not on my numbers.

CALEB
Your numbers are safe. The towel's doing security.

He fits the small boards together, marks a joint.

MARIAN
I need to work there.

CALEB
Give me a minute.

She gives him less than a minute. He moves the boards to his knees. She sits with her ledger.

MARIAN
You don't have to make me something.

CALEB
I'm making a shelf.

MARIAN
You know what I mean.

He works on the stubborn joint.

CALEB
I didn't like the way he looked at me when I couldn't tell him the dates.

MARIAN
Which one?

CALEB
Me. In the window.

Marian sets down her pencil.

CALEB
I could see myself saying it. I wanted to sound like I had everything.

MARIAN
You had what you had.

CALEB
Doesn't always make a good speech.

She takes a clean sheet. Writes HOURS at the top. Slides it to him.

MARIAN
No speech. Start with Friday.

He puts aside the shelf. Writes. Hesitates, marks a question beside an entry.

She takes up her own work. Two pages, different jobs.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM - PRE-DAWN #36#

An impressive investigation of very little.

Callie has copied names, circled structures, arranged objects. Nothing on the table says what she has said.

On the laptop: a draft email to the firm about the unrelated referral. An estate note has crept into a separate document.

She reads: UNDISCLOSED SECOND FAMILY.

Deletes UNDISCLOSED. Stops. Selects the rest. Deletes it too.

Her phone has no answer from Sarah.

From her old family folder she takes an earlier copy of an estate document. Her name appears beside a residue clause. She places it by tonight's scan. Two attestation-page images have different margins and marks.

She enlarges them. Checks the file names. Opens an email as if she is about to write a conclusion.

Instead, a new page:

WHICH COPY? WHEN? WHO HAS ORIGINALS?

Below it: WHAT CAN I ASK FOR?

Her hands stop. She looks at the family photograph Leah chose.

She moves it out of the row of documents. Stands it against the water pitcher.

It does not become evidence.

INT. MORI HOUSE - CALLIE'S OLD ROOM - DAWN #37#

The room has been a guest room long enough to lose its grudges. Callie's bag lies open on the bed.

She tries to fit the blue folder, her laptop and Ruth's food into it. No room.

Takes out the shirt meant for the journey home. Hangs it in the closet.

She leaves a message on another phone line. Her voice has lost the podium.

CALLIE
Hello. Callie Mori. I'd like to arrange an appointment about my father's estate. I have a personal interest, so I need someone independent.

She looks at the two copies on her screen.

CALLIE
I have an older copy and scans I can't reconcile. I'd like someone to look at what they actually establish, including where things stand and whether there's any basis for a challenge. I'm not asking you to file anything. Please let me know what you need before we meet.

She leaves her number. Ends the call. No answer, no advice.

Then a text to the firm: ASSESSMENT LATER TODAY. I HAVE THE FILE.

She almost adds I'M FINE. Doesn't.

INT. MORI HOUSE - KITCHEN - MORNING #38#

Ruth finds Callie at the counter holding two eggs, apparently in need of instructions.

RUTH
You're still here.

CALLIE
That's how eggs are usually made. By someone present.

Ruth looks at the open drawer where the plaque used to be.

CALLIE
I was finding the whisk.

RUTH
Eli moved it.

CALLIE
Naturally.

Ruth takes it from a jar. Gives it to her.

CALLIE
How do you want them?

RUTH
Not wet.

CALLIE
I remember.

RUTH
You asked.

Callie cracks an egg. A little shell falls in. She works it out with the edge of the larger shell.

CALLIE
Will you think about a visit to the storage unit? With Leon. Just a visit.

RUTH
I'll think about what I can agree to. I can't give you everybody.

CALLIE
I know.

Ruth waits, testing whether a second argument follows. It doesn't. Not yet.

RUTH
There are plates in the usual place.

Callie opens the correct cupboard. A small competence, finally useful.

EXT. MORI HOUSE - FRONT STEP - MORNING #39#

Callie's phone vibrates. A new voicemail.

She steps outside to listen. The morning is offensively ordinary. A neighbor pulls a bin to the curb.

SARAH (V.O.)
Callie. Sarah Caldwell. I got your message.

Callie straightens, ready to answer a voice that cannot hear her.

SARAH (V.O.)
Ruth giving you my number was not permission to tell me what my family is.

Callie closes her eyes.

SARAH (V.O.)
I might be willing to meet privately. If you can start by asking what I want from a meeting. Not by telling me how you'll handle it.

A little background sound at Sarah's end: a kettle, a cupboard. Another person's morning.

SARAH (V.O.)
I'm speaking for myself. Don't put Daniel's name on an invitation I haven't made. Send a text if you want to ask. I have work.

The message ends.

Callie types: YOU MISUNDERSTOOD.

Looks at it. Deletes.

Types: WHAT WOULD YOU WANT FROM A MEETING?

She almost adds an explanation. Sends before she can.

Inside, Ruth knocks on the window. Points at the stove. Callie holds up one finger.

Ruth holds up a spatula. She has her own deadline.

INT. MORI HOUSE - DINING ROOM / CALDWELL KITCHEN - MORNING / NIGHT (1965) #40#

Callie returns to the table. The old heading still says OTHER FAMILY?

She deletes it. Types DANIEL CALDWELL. SARAH CALDWELL.

Leaves the space below blank.

The pen rests in its sleeve. She takes it, starts to remove the cap. Stops. Sets it down unopened.

In the Caldwell kitchen, Caleb presses the last joint of the little shelf into place. It is not entirely level.

Marian puts away her ledger. Their separate household sheet remains on the table, Caleb's uncertain dates marked with questions.

She tests the shelf with a cup. It holds.

MARIAN
It leans.

CALEB
So does the house. I matched it.

She leaves the cup. A small space waits for books.

The twins sleep. Caleb and Marian stand there a moment, neither moving first and ruining it.

At the Mori table, the family photograph leans against the pitcher. Callie adjusts it so it will not slip.

Her phone is silent. She leaves it facedown.

RUTH (O.S.)
Callie. They're getting wet.

CALLIE
I'm coming.

She takes the photograph with her, leaves the papers where they are.

The pen. Two names. A question without the answer she wanted.

FADE OUT.

THE END
